---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/sections/PageHeader.astro';
import RelatedContent from '../../components/sections/RelatedContent.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/sections/CTABand.astro';
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildArticleSchema, buildFAQSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Retail Display Boxes: Plan Product Load, Facings and Replenishment';
const description = 'Plan counter and shelf display boxes around product load, facings, replenishment, artwork, barcodes and retail setup before requesting a quote.';
const url = '/blog/retail-display-boxes-for-counter-and-shelf-programs-2026-09-17';
const datePublished = '2026-09-17';
const dateModified = '2026-10-31';
const image = '/images/blog/corrugated-shipping-boxes-heavy-ecommerce-banner-v2.webp';

const breadcrumb = buildBreadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/blog' },
  { name: 'Retail Display Boxes', url }
]);
const article = {
  ...buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url, datePublished, author: 'Apex Packaging Solutions' }),
  dateModified
};
const faq = buildFAQSchema([
  {
    q: 'Should I choose a counter display or a shelf display first?',
    a: 'Start with the retailer location, available footprint, product dimensions, required facings and replenishment method. Those constraints should determine the display format rather than the format being chosen in isolation.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How many product facings should a display have?',
    a: 'Use the retailer planogram or merchandising objective together with unit dimensions, packed load and replenishment frequency. There is no universal facing count that works for every product or store.'
  },
  {
    q: 'When should barcode placement be checked?',
    a: 'Check it on the assembled, loaded display before production. Confirm the barcode type, quiet zones, print surface, orientation and intended scanning point with the responsible retailer or GS1 guidance.'
  }
]);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[breadcrumb, article, faq]}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Knowledge base · Retail displays"
    heading="Retail display boxes: plan product load, facings and replenishment."
    lead="Define how the display is loaded, opened, presented and restocked before approving its structure or artwork."
    imageSrc={image}
    imageAlt="Retail display planning materials showing product load, artwork panels and replenishment details."
  />

  <section class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="container max-w-default prose-apex">
      <p><strong>A retail display box should be designed around a specific product load and store task.</strong> The buyer needs to define the unit dimensions, packed weight, number of facings, opening sequence, available counter or shelf space and replenishment method. Those inputs affect the front opening, wall height, product support, artwork panels and whether the display travels inside a separate shipping case.</p>
      <p>Use this guide to prepare a display brief. For a purchasing enquiry, review Apex’s <a href="/industries/display-boxes">custom display boxes</a> page and send the product details, intended retail location, quantity, artwork status and delivery destination.</p>

      <figure class="my-10 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-card">
        <img src={image} alt="Retail display planning materials with cartons, product-loading notes and artwork references." width="1600" height="720" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">The approved display should connect the store footprint to a repeatable product load and setup sequence.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Choose the format from the retail task</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Format to evaluate</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Questions to resolve</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Counter display tray</td><td>A compact assortment is placed near a till or service counter</td><td>Footprint, viewing angle, product removal and staff replenishment</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shelf-ready display</td><td>The loaded unit moves onto a shelf with limited store handling</td><td>Outer-case removal, shelf depth, front lip, facings and remaining-unit stability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shipper-display system</td><td>The display also performs part of the distribution task</td><td>Transit protection, opening instructions, store setup and disposal of transport components</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>These are planning categories, not interchangeable specifications. The retailer’s planogram, handling rules and distribution route should control the final structure.</p>

      <h2>Define the complete product load</h2>
      <p>Record each unit’s maximum dimensions and packed weight, then specify the assortment and count loaded into one display. Include product variation, primary-pack friction, hanging features and any surfaces that should remain visible. A display that holds one sample may behave differently when fully loaded or partly depleted.</p>
      <p>State the required facings and the order in which staff or shoppers remove products. Product movement can block the front panel, allow units to rotate or make the final units difficult to reach. Review the full, half-full and nearly empty conditions rather than only the launch photo.</p>

      <h2>Write the setup and replenishment sequence</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Describe whether the display arrives preloaded, flat or inside an outer case.</li>
        <li>Identify every tear strip, removable panel, fold, lock or packing component used during setup.</li>
        <li>Record the expected orientation on the counter or shelf.</li>
        <li>Show how staff replenish the display without damaging artwork or mixing SKUs.</li>
        <li>Define what happens to transport material after setup.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A short trial with the people who will pack and replenish the unit can reveal unclear instructions, slow assembly or a front opening that does not work with the loaded product.</p>

      <h2>Place artwork around the assembled display</h2>
      <p>Map the primary viewing panel, price or promotion area, product windows and any retailer-required label zones on the assembled structure. Artwork should use the final dieline revision so opening features and removable panels do not cut through important copy.</p>
      <p>Barcode placement needs a separate check. GS1 Canada advises preserving quiet zones, keeping symbols away from package edges and using a smooth surface. A trade item sold in a different packaging level or configuration may also need its own identifier. Confirm the responsible barcode and scan point before files are released.</p>

      <h2>Decide how the display travels</h2>
      <p>A display may travel loaded inside an outer case, perform as part of the shipper or be assembled at the store. Each route exposes it to different handling and compression conditions. Define the entire packed system, not only the visible tray, and select validation from the real distribution environment. ISTA’s official guidance emphasizes choosing tests with knowledge of that environment rather than using one test for every package.</p>
      <p><strong>Hypothetical example:</strong> a six-facing counter tray is stable when full but the last two units tip after repeated shopper handling. The correction might involve the product arrangement or internal support; adding board thickness without reviewing the load does not necessarily solve the buyer’s problem.</p>

      <h2>Retail display RFQ checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Product drawings, photos, unit dimensions, unit weight and permitted variation.</li>
        <li>SKU assortment, units per display, facings and replenishment sequence.</li>
        <li>Retailer footprint, shelf or counter constraints and planogram requirements.</li>
        <li>Preloaded, flat-packed or shipper-display delivery method.</li>
        <li>Outer-case and pallet information where the display travels through distribution.</li>
        <li>Final artwork status, viewing priorities, labels and barcode requirements.</li>
        <li>Quantity by version, delivery destinations and required approval stages.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Should I choose a counter display or a shelf display first?</h3>
      <p>Start with the retailer location, available footprint, product dimensions, required facings and replenishment method. Those constraints should determine the format.</p>
      <h3>How many product facings should a display have?</h3>
      <p>Use the planogram or merchandising objective together with the unit dimensions, full load and replenishment frequency. There is no universal facing count.</p>
      <h3>When should barcode placement be checked?</h3>
      <p>Check it on the assembled, loaded display before production. Confirm the barcode type, quiet zones, print surface, orientation and intended scanning point.</p>

      <h2>Primary references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.gs1ca.org/barcodes/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GS1 Canada: barcode placement, quiet zones and verification</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gs1ca.org/standards/global-trade-item-numbers/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GS1 Canada: GTINs for products and packaging levels</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.ista.org/getting_started_with_design.php" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISTA: distribution-aware package design and testing</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RelatedContent items={[
    { title: 'Custom Display Boxes', href: '/industries/display-boxes', description: 'Review the commercial display-box page for a purchasing enquiry.' },
    { title: 'Retail Display Format Guide', href: '/blog/retail-display-packaging-counter-trays-pdq-guide', description: 'Compare counter trays and shelf-ready display formats.' },
    { title: 'Packaging RFQ Template', href: '/blog/custom-packaging-rfq-template-12-specs-buyers-include', description: 'Organize the specifications needed for a comparable quote.' }
  ]} />

  <CTABand
    heading="Quote the display around the real retail task."
    lead="Send the product load, retail footprint, setup method, artwork status, quantity and destination for a display-box quotation."
    primaryCta={{ label: 'Request a display-box quote', href: '/contact' }}
  />
</BaseLayout>
